= Basic =
alias Question to Q
alias Answer to A

(Q) What is the most basic of regular expressions?
(A) The single literal character, which matches the first occurence of it in a string.

(Q) By default, regex engines match how many times?
(A) Once, the first. But for convenience, many iterate through every match.

(Q) What are the twelve characters with special meaning in regex?
(A) Metacharacters; the backslash \, the caret ^, the dollar sign $, the period or dot ., the vertical bar or pipe symbol |, the question mark ?, the asterisk or star *, the plus sign +, the opening parenthesis (, the closing parenthesis ), the opening square bracket [, and the opening curly brace {.

(Q) How to match unicode characters if it's not supported in the syntax?
(A) Use \xFF to match a character by its hexadecimal index in the character set.

(Q) A character class matches?
(A) Only one out of several characters, it's a choice branch. gr[ae]y matches either gray or grey.

(Q) Within character classes, how do you match a range of characters?
(A) With a dash joining two points (increasing) of the set. They can be compounded by placing more next to another.

(Q) Character sets can include?
(A) Ranges, single characters, or BOTH.

(Q) You can negate character classes by?
(A) Using a caret after the opening square bracket.

(Q) \d matches?
(A) A single character that's a digit. Usually includes many different writing systems.

(Q) \w matches?
(A) A word character (alphanumeric including underscore)

(Q) \b matches?
(A) At a word boundary, which is anything in-between a character that can be matched by \w and a character that cannot be matched by \w.

(Q) What is alternation?
(A) The regular expression equivalent of "or"

(Q) The question mark makes?
(A) The preceding token optional. colou?r matches colour or color.

(Q) An asterisk tells the engine?
(A) To attempt to match the preceding token zero or more times.

(Q) What is the practical reason to use *?
(A) For dealing with optional structures, like unrequired whitespace. BUT where the amount of the optional is irrelevant (like a ton of space). 

(Q) If you want precise control over the amount of repition, use?
(A) A number between curly braces, following the token you want to repeat. \b[1-9][0-9]{3}\b matches an number with an inital digit of 1-9, followed by THREE digits 0-9.

(Q) Parentheses create?
(A) A capturing group.

(Q) Back reference \1 matches? 
(A) The same text matched by the capturing group it corresponds to. (So the first one in this case)

(Q) The question mark makes?
(A) The preceding token optional, runs if it exists, or if it doesn't, but doesn't match more than once.

(Q) A plus tells the engine?
(A) To attempt to match the preceding token once or more?

(Q) The dot matches?
(A) Any single character, EXCEPT line break characters.



